    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the earlier confusion! I was trying to use a goblin analogy to explain how a complex signal is actually just a sum of simple sine waves, but I got carried away with the goblin lore.\n\nTo answer your math question directly: A signal \"contains\" frequencies because any complex wave can be mathematically decomposed into a sum of pure sine and cosine waves. Think of it like this: if you have a complicated wave, you can imagine it being built by adding together many simple sine waves of different heights (amplitudes) and speeds (frequencies).\n\nThe Fourier Transform is the tool that calculates exactly how much of each specific frequency is needed to reconstruct that original signal. So, when you \"break it down,\" you aren't physically tearing the signal apart; you are finding the specific coefficients (the weights) for each frequency component that, when added together, perfectly recreate the original signal. It's essentially solving for the unique combination of pure tones that make up your messy signal.",
